 "title": "A follow up call must have a reason",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "The creator explains that following up based on fixed day cycles like day one or day five is a wrong approach. Instead, the date, time, location, and specific intent of the next conversation must be decided before ending the current call.",
 "key_points": [
  "Most people make the mistake of using rigid follow-up schedules like day one, day two, day three, etc.",
  "The creator claims that following fixed follow-up cycles is a wrong approach.",
  "The date, time, location, and intent of the next conversation must be decided before ending the current call.",
  "Example reason to state for a call: 'The reason we are going to get on a call Tuesday at 2 p.m. is because we are going to make a decision on whether or not we are moving forward with this particular thing.'",
  "The reason for the next call must be extremely clear and specific, not vague."
 ],
 "content": "A follow up call must have a reason. One of the most common mistakes that most people do with follow ups is when do we know we should follow up? That's a very wrong approach. It's like assuming there is a parameter to follow, Day one, Day three, Day four, Day five, Day six. So there is nothing called oh, this is a follow up cycle. That means on day one this has to go out and on day five this has to go out. The general rule of thumb is the first time you talk to them, the next conversations date, time, location, and intent of conversation, that is the reason to get on that call, needs to be decided before you get off one call. So when you get off a call, be like, hey, the reason we are going to get on a call Tuesday at 2 p.m. is because we are going to make a decision on whether or not we are moving forward with this particular thing. They need to say yes for that. It has to be extremely clear specific. It is not vague. [On-screen text: A follow up call must have a reason. Follow up Protocol. Clips from build100 link in bio]",
 "status": "done",
